    # Suggested fix action: Replace the floor assertion with a set-equality assertion between the discovered population and the declared table, whose message prints both counts and both directions of the difference. Emit it ONCE for the whole population rather than once per member, so the reader gets the list instead of N single names. THE OBVIOUS WRONG GUARD, and one is already in the tree: asserting the declared table against ITS OWN length. That is self-consistency; it passes for any table and can never see the population. Assert against the POPULATION. Where several declared tables cover one discovered population, assert each against it and assert the tables agree with one another — the defect is not confined to invocation tables: the same shape sits on the present-but-empty input table and on the hand-listed producer set, whose discovered counterpart is an importable package. NOTE ON WHERE THIS LIVES: no program in this tree audits a test suite's own population arithmetic, so this routes to the gate that stands between a plugin change and a completion claim. Its natural home is a new member of the meta-gate family that already audits the program population for wiring and for a disclosed denominator; build it there and this gate calls it. COUNT THE OTHER SITES BEFORE BUILDING: measured on this tree the same shape sits on SIX tables of one layer — two invocation tables at 15 of 19, a present-but-empty input table at 8 of 19, a hand-named producer trio, a literal backend tuple whose package is importable, and the envelope-producer direction of the census at three literals. Only the first was known when this record was written; the other five came from asking the question once per table. A fix that repairs one table and leaves five is the floor again at a different altitude. CORPUS-SWEPT: the suite holds 161 floor assertions of this shape, of which 36 are over a population the file DISCOVERS and carry a bound above one — that is the rule's population and it is trac