    # Suggested fix action: Define the population as every executable that imports the layer's package or cites the layer's interface document, and take the prefix glob as one contributor rather than as the definition. Assert that the relation-derived population is a superset of the glob-derived one, and drive every arm of the layer property over the relation-derived set. NOTE ON WHERE THIS LIVES: same home as the population-equality rule, and they share a helper — the relation-derived population is the input both need, so computing it twice is how the two answers start to disagree. CORPUS-SWEPT, AND IT NEEDS A DISCRIMINATOR THE RECORD DID NOT HAVE: 32 test files select a population by filename prefix, and MOST ARE CORRECT — the universal prefix that means `every test` is exactly the right selector for a suite-wide property. The rule fires only where the prefix stands in for a SEMANTIC SUBSET, a layer or a family, because that is where the naming convention and the boundary can drift apart. Distinguish them by whether the prefix names a subset at all: the universal one has no sibling population it could be missing.
